\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf1 PHOTOS: Neosho High School in Missouri. From 2014 to 2018, eight of its 5,000 students died by suicide. (A1); Neosho is a small town on the edge of the Ozarks, the kind of place where much of the population shows up for high school football games. It is also a place where children have struggled terribly.; Talmage Clubbs, director of counseling for the Neosho School District, said he\u8217 s hesitant to suspend alerts.; Angel Cholka, who was awakened one night by a police officer who had come to check on her daughter, Madi, who had already taken about 15 pills.; Tracy Clements, former director of counseling for Neosho School District, now works for GoGuardian.; School-issued laptops, with Beacon software from GoGuardian, awaiting repair at Neosho High School. Rights organizations warn of privacy risks. (A18); The Neosho High School freshmen football team before a game against Republic High School in October. Neosho was one of GoGuardian\u8217 s first customers, beta-testing the software for a discount.; Cards produced for the funeral of Madi Cholka, who died at 17. \u8220 I\u8217 m sorry,\u8221  she wrote in a text to her mother, Angel, right, who wears a necklace bearing Madi\u8217 s fingerprints. Ms. Cholka said she was grateful for the Beacon alerts and disagrees with the arguments about students\u8217  privacy and the intrusion on families. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY GRAHAM DICKIE/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A19) This article appeared in print on page A1, A18, A19.}
